Notes on the limit_traffic_rate module
=====

To install, compile nginx with this ./configure option:

    --add-module=path/to/this/directory

Nginx directive limit_rate could limit connection's speed, and limit_conn 
could limit connection number by given variable. If the client is a browser,
 it only open one connection to the server. The speed will be limited to 
limit_rate, unless the client is a multi-thread download tool.

Limit_traffic_rate module provides a method to limit the total download rate
 by client IP or download URL, even there are several connections. The 
limit condition could be defined by the following directive.

The limit_traffic_rate module need to use a share memory pool. Directive 
syntax is same to limit_zone. 

    http {
        #limit_traffic_rate_zone   rate $request_uri 32m;
        limit_traffic_rate_zone   rate $remote_addr 32m;
        
        server {
            location /download/ {
                limit_traffic_rate  rate 20k;
            }
        }
    }

Several limit_traffic_rate directives using different zones may be given in
the same block. A request is then accounted in every zone and the lowest of
the computed rates is applied. For example, to limit each client IP to 100k
and the whole virtual server to 10m at the same time:

    http {
        limit_traffic_rate_zone   perip  $binary_remote_addr 32m;
        limit_traffic_rate_zone   global $server_name 1m;

        server {
            server_name  example.com;

            location /download/ {
                limit_traffic_rate  perip  100k;
                limit_traffic_rate  global 10m;
            }
        }
    }

Requests for which the zone variable is empty are not limited by that zone
(in the example above server_name must be set). Using the same zone twice in
one block is an error. The directives are inherited from the previous level
only if there are no limit_traffic_rate directives on the current level.

Examples
=====

The rate given to limit_traffic_rate is shared by all connections whose
zone key ($binary_remote_addr, $request_uri, $host, ...) has the same value.
It is a total, not a per-connection limit: with "limit_traffic_rate rate 4m"
one connection may get 4MB/s, while 8 connections from the same client get
about 512KB/s each.

Note: set limit_traffic_rate inside location{}. Older versions of this
module ignore it at http{}/server{} level.

1. Limit each client IP to at most 8 connections and 4MB/s in total, e.g.
   for multi-thread download managers. limit_conn is the standard nginx
   module; the 9th connection is rejected with 503.

    http {
        limit_conn_zone           $binary_remote_addr zone=addr:10m;
        limit_traffic_rate_zone   rate $binary_remote_addr 32m;

        server {
            location /download/ {
                limit_conn          addr 8;
                limit_traffic_rate  rate 4m;
            }
        }
    }

2. Limit the total download rate of each URI (all clients together), or of
   each virtual host. $uri is used rather than $request_uri, which includes
   the query string, so "?x" can not be added to get a new limit.

    http {
        limit_traffic_rate_zone   per_uri  $uri 32m;
        limit_traffic_rate_zone   per_host $host 32m;

        server {
            location /video/ {
                limit_traffic_rate  per_uri 1m;
            }
            location /files/ {
                limit_traffic_rate  per_host 10m;
            }
        }
    }

